Detection Engineering Lab – Suspicious PowerShell Execution


Detection focused on: 
• EncodedCommand usage 
• IEX (Invoke-Expression) 
• DownloadString execution

SPL Query:
index=main powershell.exe
| search "EncodedCommand" OR "DownloadString" OR "IEX"
| table _time User ParentProcessName CommandLine

• EncodedCommand: Attackers use this built-in PowerShell parameter to run commands hidden inside Base64-encoded strings. It turns human-readable script into a scrambled block of text, masking the true intent from superficial logging and basic security tools.

• DownloadString: A .NET method (Net.WebClient) used to download a script or payload from a remote URL straight into system memory. Because the payload runs dynamically in RAM, it completely bypasses traditional antivirus tools that scan files on the hard drive.

• IEX (Invoke-Expression): This cmdlet takes any string of text and executes it as a live command. Attackers heavily pair IEX with DownloadString to instantly pull a malicious script from the internet and run it in a single fluid motion.

Why it matters: Attackers frequently abuse PowerShell to execute obfuscated commands, download payloads, and evade traditional defenses.

Investigation points: 
• Parent-child process relationship
• User context 
• Command-line arguments 
• External download indicators

MITRE ATT&CK: • T1059.001 – Command and Scripting Interpreter: PowerShell
